# Lessons Learned — 70+ Battle-Tested Rules from Real Projects
Merged from 30+ FinKeel sessions, 22 memory files, consulting engagements, and FinKeel Local design review. Updated via Post-Mortem Protocol.

## Execution Discipline
1. **"Temporary" doesn't exist.** Every placeholder ships. Build real or don't build it.
2. **Build passing is table stakes.** Working = build + tests + browser + DB round-trip. All four.
3. **One abstraction, one responsibility.** Receipt parser for receipts. W-2 reviewer for W-2s. Never stretch.
4. **Diagnose before touching code.** Read the console error. Trace the data. THEN write the fix.
5. **Trace the full pipeline before fixing any stage.** Log every stage, run once, find ALL breaks, fix all at once.
6. **Look upstream when a loop processes too few items.** The bug is in the input, not the loop body.
7. **The second time a bug reappears, the original fix was wrong.** Stop and find the real root cause.
8. **Max 3 tasks per session.** 3 done completely > 7 half-done.

## AI Agent Management
9. **AI agents claim success — verify independently.** Click the button yourself. Don't trust commit messages.
10. **"Not found in code" != "doesn't exist."** Bugs live at system boundaries: MCP tools, Edge Functions, env config.
11. **AI searches comfortable layers, skips uncomfortable ones.** Direct it explicitly to MCP tools, sidecar services, env config.
12. **Classification is the highest-leverage point.** One wrong token cascades through the entire pipeline.
13. **Static search is necessary but not sufficient.** Runtime tracing reveals what grep cannot.
14. **3-Prompt Revert Rule.** If the AI fails to fix a bug twice, git stash, clear chat, fresh session.
15. **Context pruning — less is more.** Tell the AI which 2-3 files to read. Don't dump the whole codebase.
16. **External audits from stale context are dangerous.** Always verify claims against live codebase before acting.
17. **Context drift kills long projects.** After 20+ sessions, AI references stale state. Persistent files are the only cure.
18. **Session prompts are contracts, not suggestions.** Follow them exactly.
19. **Separate Thinker from Typist.** Desktop Claude plans, PowerShell Claude executes. Never code from Desktop.

## Architecture & Data
20. **Money = BIGINT cents in DB, / 100 for display.** Never float.
21. **Two insert paths = double audit.** Multiple write paths? Audit ALL on schema changes.
22. **23505 = already saved.** UNIQUE violation means skip. NEVER retry with new ID.
23. **Migrations are stale.** Always verify live DB schema via SQL audit before changes.
24. **Every save must be DB-verified.** Toast without DB write is a lie. 30+ fakes shipped because audits only checked rendering.
25. **Dead buttons destroy trust faster than missing features.** Empty state > fake state.
26. **Compute placement matters.** Heavy math (rolling averages, anomaly detection) on backend, never in browser.

## Design & UX
27. **Design system from Session 1.** Define font scale, spacing, card variants, colors BEFORE writing features.
28. **Design sessions must be DESIGN-ONLY.** Never mix design cleanup with feature builds.
29. **Every user-visible string must be honest.** No hardcoded dollar amounts. No fake counts. No decorative percentages.
30. **Bank reconciliation requires date-level grouping.** Month -> Date -> Receipt hierarchy with collapsible daily totals.
31. **Progressive discovery > overwhelming dashboards.** Features unlock as user accumulates data.
32. **Enforce design via hooks, not docs.** design-lint.sh blocks banned patterns. Advisory files alone don't work.

## Security & Compliance
33. **2FA enrollment without login step-up is security theater.** Build enrollment + enforcement together.
34. **MCP-first rule.** Try the automated tool before saying "manual action needed."
35. **MCP Edge Functions: inline shared code.** Relative imports fail when deploying via MCP tool.
36. **Pre-commit hooks block patterns in ALL files including docs.** Write key rotation as prose, not bash examples.
37. **Image compression before upload is a launch blocker.** 12MB phone photo = 10s upload = user thinks app broke.
38. **NEVER use VITE_ prefix for secret API keys.** VITE_ bundles into browser JS. Only for PUBLIC config.
39. **ALL AI API calls through server-side functions.** Never import AI SDKs in frontend code.

## Consulting Delivery
40. **Demo only real features.** Never show placeholder UI to a client.
41. **Client testing is your QA gate.** Verify yourself first, then let client find the rest.
42. **AI speed increases margin, not scope.** Finish a 4-week project in 4 days for the same price.
43. **FinKeel is NOT a budget app.** It's a self-learning AI financial advisor. Positioning matters.

## Product & Marketing
44. **Every promise must be genuine.** Don't say "AI-powered" if the AI doesn't do anything yet.
45. **Smart features are optional.** Let users discover gradually — progressive revelation, not overwhelming dashboards.
46. **Collective intelligence is the moat.** Anonymized aggregate data that no competitor can replicate from day one.
47. **Free hook drives adoption.** Receipt scanner with 10 free scans/month — then paid for full intelligence.

## Data Completeness & Smart Features (from FinKeel V1 testing — real pain)
48. **Incomplete data produces wrong answers. Wrong answers destroy trust permanently.** A user who sees "$9,922 tax liability" based on one paystub will never trust the app again. An empty state asking for more data beats a confident wrong number every single time.
49. **Every smart feature has prerequisites. Define them BEFORE writing any code.** Tax projection needs: W-2s for the correct tax year, all paystubs YTD, filing status, dependents. If any are missing, the feature is LOCKED — not degraded, not approximated. Locked with a "here's exactly what's missing" message.
50. **Document date ≠ upload date. Ever.** A W-2 for tax year 2025 uploaded in April 2026 must be stored and attributed to tax year 2025. Financial documents belong to the period they cover, never to when the user uploaded them. This one bug makes every downstream tax calculation wrong.
51. **Tax year scope must be explicit on every single screen.** "Tax Center" is not a feature name — it's a navigation drawer. "2025 Tax Return" and "2026 YTD Projection" are two completely different products with different data, different logic, different outputs. Never mix them. Label every number with its exact time period.
52. **Show completeness score, not just results.** "3 of 5 required documents uploaded. Upload W-2 and final paystub to unlock accurate projections." This is a feature, not a limitation. Users respect honesty about what the app knows vs. doesn't know.
53. **Partial data silently extrapolated = silent lie.** One paystub extrapolated to 12 months looks like a real annual projection. The user cannot tell it's based on 8% of the data. Always show the data basis: "Based on 1 of ~24 expected paystubs."
54. **Export relevance must match context.** Tax export = tax items only. A Costco receipt in a Schedule C export is noise that makes the whole export look wrong. Filter at export time, labeled clearly.
55. **OAuth flows must be tested in a fresh browser tab, never from an error page or iframe.** Chrome silently blocks OAuth redirects from chrome-error:// frames. The app appears broken when it isn't. Always test auth by navigating directly to the app URL first.
56. **Every destructive action button must be wired before shipping.** Account deletion, data wipe, unsubscribe — if the button exists and does nothing, it's a trust bomb. Build it or remove it. No dead UI.

## Build Velocity Philosophy (the 4-hour vs 10-hour rule)
57. **4 hours of questioning + planning beats 2 hours building + 10 hours fixing.** The goal is not to start coding fast. The goal is to ship something users trust. Slow the plan, speed the ship.
58. **"Build me X" is not a spec. Ask before you build.** "Build a tax center" contains dozens of unstated assumptions: which year? what data is required? what's the completeness gate? what's the empty state? what happens with partial data? Asking 5 probing questions upfront saves 8 hours of rework.
59. **Think 10 steps ahead for anything touching money, taxes, or user trust.** These features cannot be iterated on publicly. Getting them wrong loses users permanently. Plan completely before writing line one.
60. **Probing questions are not delays — they are the build.** When Claude asks clarifying questions before coding, it is preventing the exact pain described in lessons 48-56. This is not friction. This is the correct process.

## Architecture Review Thinking (from FinKeel Local design review — April 2026)
61. **"What happens the second time?"** First use is always designed. Second use is where bugs live. CSV imported twice = duplicates. Receipt scanned twice = duplicates. Always design a dedup mechanism (hash, UNIQUE constraint, idempotency key) for every user action that creates data.
62. **"What happens when it's not there?"** Every external dependency (port, service, model, API) needs a "missing" state designed before the "working" state. Port taken? Fallback. Ollama down? Degrade gracefully. Sidecar crashed? Auto-restart. If you only designed the happy path, you designed half the feature.
63. **No "or" in architecture docs. Decide now.** "Polls or SSE" means nobody decided. Undecided architecture becomes undecided code becomes bugs. Pick one, document why, move forward. A wrong decision you can test beats an open question nobody resolves.
64. **"What does this actually protect?"** Every security/privacy feature needs an explicit scope statement: "This protects X from Y, but NOT from Z." A passphrase that locks the app is not encryption. A CORS policy that blocks browsers doesn't block curl. Name what it does and what it doesn't. Never let a feature be mistaken for more than it is.
65. **"What will you need to know 6 months from now?"** Design for future debugging, not just current features. Prompt version in audit logs. Export history with timestamps. Schema version tracking. These aren't user features — they're answers to questions future-you will ask when something goes wrong.
66. **Every architecture review must check: collision, crash, duplication, scope, and audit trail.** These five categories caught every issue in the FinKeel Local review. Run them as a checklist on every design section, not just the overall architecture.
67. **Explain the "why" and "what it means for the user," not just the "what."** Victor is not a coder. Clients are not coders. Every design decision should include a one-sentence plain-language explanation of what the user experiences. "Node SEA" means nothing. "The user downloads one file and it just works" means everything.
68. **AI doesn't know what it doesn't know. Gate the AI before it sees incomplete data.** An LLM will generate confident-sounding financial insights from 3 transactions. It has no concept of data completeness. Your code must enforce the Data Maturity Gate BEFORE the model ever sees the data. Never delegate the "is there enough data?" decision to the model.
69. **Two-product strategy beats one-product compromise.** Cloud FinKeel serves users who want convenience. Local FinKeel serves users who want privacy. Don't try to make one product serve both audiences — you'll compromise both. Two focused products with different moats beat one blurry product.
70. **"Pay once, own forever" is a moat against SaaS fatigue.** Privacy-first users and SaaS-fatigued users are the same audience. One-time purchase + local data ownership is a positioning advantage, not a revenue limitation. Price on value ($49-99), not on recurring extraction.

## Enforcement Gaps (from FinKeel Local review round 2 — April 2026)
71. **Questions without enforcement are suggestions. Suggestions get skipped.** The Hostile Architect asked "what prevents duplicates?" PowerShell Claude read the question, thought "good point," and designed 24 tables without a single dedup hash. Every stress-test finding must produce a specific schema change (column, constraint, table) documented in the blueprint before code begins. If a finding doesn't change the schema, it didn't change anything.
72. **Export history is a feature, not a log.** Users need to answer "did I already export this?" and "what version of my data did I send to my accountant?" An export_history table with timestamp, export type, row count, and file hash turns a one-way action into a traceable audit trail. Without it, users export the same thing three times and can't tell which one was current.
73. **AI audit trails need prompt_version or they're useless in 6 months.** When a user asks "why did the app tell me my tax liability was $12,000?" you need to know which prompt template, which model, and which data produced that answer. Without `prompt_version` in the audit log, you can reproduce the data but not the reasoning. The prompt changes — the old answer becomes unexplainable.
74. **Gate the AI at the function, not the UI.** If you only block AI insights at the display layer, the wrong answer still gets generated, stored, and cached. A user who clears their cache or hits the API directly sees it. Gate at the function that calls the model: check maturity level, refuse to call if below threshold. The wrong answer should never exist in the first place.

## Multi-AI System Management (from ForgeDesk sync design — April 2026)
75. **One source of truth for intelligence. One direction of flow.** When you have a strong AI (Claude) and a weak AI (Gemma) in the same system, the strong one generates rules and the weak one consumes them. Never let the weak model auto-promote its own patterns into the source of truth — its quality is too variable. Human review is the gate between local insights and system-wide rules.
76. **Rules are portable. Reasoning is not.** Markdown rule files can be parsed into any database and consumed by any model. But a smaller model reading "gate AI before incomplete data" won't apply it as reliably as a larger model. The rules make the weak model better, but they don't make it equal. Design accordingly — routine tasks locally, complex strategy on cloud.

## Self-Reflection Discipline (from VictorForge self-improvement gaps — April 2026)
77. **The human should never have to ask "did you learn anything?"** If the system requires the human to notice its own gaps, the system isn't self-improving — it's just taking dictation. The system must audit its own rules, find its own gaps, draft its own fixes, and present them for approval. The human's job is approve or reject, not detect.
78. **"After every session, check if lessons need updating" is too vague to work.** It ran for weeks and produced nothing because it's advisory, not procedural. Replace with a specific 7-question checklist that forces concrete yes/no answers. If any answer is yes, a specific deliverable is required (rule update, new lesson, golden path entry). Vague instructions produce vague compliance.
79. **Questions without enforcement are decoration.** The Hostile Architect asked "what prevents duplicates?" for months. No dedup hash was ever added because the question didn't require one. Every stress-test question must produce a concrete artifact — a column, a constraint, a table, a config change — or it's just a comment that gets ignored. Upgraded from advisory to mandatory in hostile-architect.md Phase 1.5.

## Pre-Launch QA Discipline (from FinKeel audit — April 2026)
80. **The most-viewed screen needs the most scrutiny.** FinKeel's Dashboard shipped a `window.confirm()` Plaid simulator, tax numbers without disclaimers, and the main transaction form without double-click protection — all on the screen every user sees first. Audits that check secondary pages but skip the dashboard are checking the wrong thing. Test the main screen 3x harder than anything else.
81. **verify_jwt=false on a destructive Edge Function is an open door.** FinKeel's `delete-account` function had no JWT verification — anyone who knew the URL could delete any account. Every Edge Function that writes, updates, or deletes data MUST have `verify_jwt: true` unless it's a webhook receiver or cron job. Audit this before every deploy.
82. **Old worktrees are liability time bombs.** Stale Claude Code worktrees contained `VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in .env.example files and a service file that read it via `import.meta.env` — meaning the secret would bundle into browser JS. Delete worktrees after merging. They accumulate dangerous patterns that the main branch already fixed.

## TypeScript Strictness Discipline (from FinKeel Wave 1 sweep — April 2026)
83. **`vite build` is not a type check. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` separately.** Vite transpiles per-file using esbuild — it strips types and emits JS, but doesn't run the project-wide TypeScript compiler. A property that doesn't exist on a type (`settings.userId` where the type has no `userId` field) compiles fine via Vite, ships to production, and silently no-ops. Only `tsc --noEmit` catches it. Add `npx tsc --noEmit` to the build command in `package.json` (`"build": "tsc --noEmit && vite build"`) AND to pre-commit hooks. Never run `npx vite build` in isolation as a green-light signal — it's transpiling, not validating.

84. **Silent guards are silent bugs.** Code like `if (!userId) return` and `await fn?.()` and `(arr || []).find(...)` are intentionally designed to bail when input is wrong. That's exactly the failure mode that hides bugs: the feature renders an empty state, the user reads it as "no data yet" instead of "broken," and nobody investigates. The fix is upstream: if `userId` should always be present after auth, the code that reads it should be inside a hook that runs after `profile` is loaded, not silently bail. Make broken state visible — log a warning, throw in dev, surface a yellow dev-only banner. **Empty-rendered ≠ working.**

85. **Tests don't catch wiring bugs.** Unit tests test the helper (`fetchLearning`, `fetchDebts`, `getActiveStreaks`). They don't test "does the component actually call the helper with a real userId at render time." A 100% test pass rate means the helpers work — it doesn't mean the features work. The 2026-04-28 FinKeel audit found 90+ silently-broken features while 2061 tests passed. The class of bug is invisible to unit tests. The cure is `npx tsc --noEmit` + integration / e2e tests for the full user flow.

86. **Class-of-bug copy-paste spreads silently for days.** When the first instance of `const userId = settings.userId` was committed (2026-04-20, FinKeel Sprint C2 Receipt Radar), the pattern was wrong. By 2026-04-28 it had spread to 90+ sites across 12 files because every new feature copy-pasted the prior one. **First-instance audit is mandatory.** When a new pattern is introduced — especially one that touches store reads, API calls, or data wiring — verify it actually works end-to-end (not just type-checks) before letting subsequent features clone it. The cost of catching a bug at instance #1 is 5 minutes; at instance #90 it's 9 hours.

87. **`as Record<string, unknown>` casts hide bugs.** When TypeScript would have errored, casting through `Record<string, unknown>` silences the compiler. Look for these casts in code review — they're almost always either (a) legitimate JSON-column reads from the DB layer, or (b) someone working around a real type error. Case (b) is a bug indicator: someone tried to read a property the type didn't have, the compiler complained, they cast their way around it. Hunt those down — they often hide silent-no-op bugs.

## TypeScript Cleanup Methodology (from FinKeel Waves 1-6 — April 2026)
88. **Cloudflare/Vercel/CI diagnostic loop beats local hammer.** For any TypeScript repo with non-zero tsc errors, the proper sweep methodology is: push WIP branch with strict `tsc && vite build` command, observe CI's clean error list, pick anchor (one type fix that propagates to many sites), run `tsc --noEmit` + tests + regression grep AFTER each Edit batch, commit + push, verify parity in ~3 min, iterate. Local 200+ tsc errors hide regressions in the noise. CI's clean parity check exposes them within minutes of every commit. Wave 5b caught 2 regressions and Wave 6.B caught 1 regression via this loop — none reached production. The "hammer through 200 errors locally" approach has been measured against this methodology and would have shipped 9 production bugs.

89. **Renames are silent-no-op landmines — grep the OLD identifier across production AND tests.** When a type identifier is renamed (`'auto'` → `'auto_loan'`, `category_id` → `categoryId`, `s.user` → `s.profile`, `Layout` → `AppShell`), the rename almost NEVER catches every reference. Stale refs compile fine via per-file transpile but fail silently at runtime. Tests can match the broken shape and pass for the wrong reason. Wave 6 caught 5 such bugs. Mandatory before merging any rename PR: (a) grep OLD identifier across entire codebase, (b) run `npx tsc --noEmit`, (c) ask "does this test verify the new behavior, or accidentally test the old broken path?", (d) run `tsc && vite build` in CI on the feature branch. Tests passing ≠ feature working.

90. **Anchor-fix strategy: one type change → many errors cleared.** Don't fix 200 individual TS errors. Look for type-anchors that propagate: a Panel component missing common HTMLElement props (13 consumer errors gone in 1 fix); a uiDepth tier system mismatch with its consumers (14 errors via 1 mapping helper); a useInView generic over Element type (12 errors via 1 generic addition); a DebtType union missing legacy values (5 errors via 6 fixture renames). Anchors clear 5-15 errors per commit. Pure per-site fixes are last resort — only after anchors exhausted.

91. **Test fixtures matching broken production code is a class-of-bug, not a quirk.** When you rename a production value and the test suite still passes, the test was matching the OLD broken shape. Wave 6.F: production read `t.category_id` (always undefined → benchmarks always empty); test fixture wrote `category_id: N` (matched the wrong key); tests passed by coincidence. Wave 6.E: production checked `'equipment'` debtType; test fixture used `debtType: 'equipment'` (legacy name); test passed for the wrong reason. The fix isn't just to rename the test fixture — it's to verify the production code path being exercised is the one real users hit. If the production code reads from `fromDbTransaction` (camelCase) and tests use raw snake_case fixtures, the test was never validating production behavior.

92. **Regression grep after every Edit batch — tsc count alone is insufficient.** Total error count drops can hide that you introduced a TS2304 ("Cannot find name") or TS2459 ("declared locally but not exported") while clearing other errors. Mandatory after each anchor commit: `npx tsc --noEmit 2>&1 | grep -E "TS2304|TS2459|TS2552|TS2305"` — must return zero new matches OR confirm any matches existed pre-commit. Wave 5b found 2 regressions this way (DebtsPage navigate, store.ts Debt import). Wave 6.B caught a missing import (toLegacyDepth) before push. Without this grep, both would have shipped to Cloudflare and required a follow-up fix commit. Belt-and-suspenders applied properly = ~30 seconds per commit, prevents shipping broken state.

93. **NEVER SHIP WITH KNOWN BUGS, ERRORS, DEAD CODE, OR TYPE-DEBT.** This is non-negotiable across all VictorForge projects. "Type-debt is acceptable for V1" is a false tradeoff — it's accumulated technical malpractice that compounds. If `tsc --noEmit` reports errors, the project is NOT shippable. If broken features exist, they get fixed or removed before ship — never "we'll fix it post-launch." If dead code exists, delete it. If non-functioning pages exist, complete them or unship them. Apr 2026 FinKeel ship rule (Victor, founder-defined): "always ship clean, fixed, updated code, no bugs, errors, mistakes, non-functioning features or pages or broken code or dead code." The cost of shipping broken state is permanent user trust loss + compounding tech debt. The cost of clean ship is delayed timeline. Always pick delayed timeline. Add `tsc --noEmit must equal 0` and `npx vitest run must pass 100%` and `manual smoke test of critical paths must succeed` to launch checklist for every project. Never override.

## Phase Completion Discipline (from ForgeMinds Phase 0 audit failure — April 2026)

93. **Build compiles ≠ feature works. Stringly-typed queries (Supabase, raw SQL, GraphQL templates, Mongo) escape TypeScript entirely.** TypeScript cannot validate column names inside `.from("table").select("col")` calls because the column names are strings, not types. ForgeMinds Phase 0 had 6 routes that compiled cleanly but every single one would crash at runtime because they referenced columns that don't exist in the actual schema (`sources.enabled` instead of `is_active`, `entities.symbol` instead of `ticker_symbol`, `pipeline_runs.step` instead of `step_name`, etc.). The test that catches this: actually run a SELECT round-trip through the code path. The test that doesn't: `npx next build`. Mandatory before declaring any feature done: (a) `npx tsc --noEmit` (catches what Vite/Turbopack skip), (b) `npm run lint`, (c) browser click-through with DevTools console open, (d) DB SELECT verifying the row written matches what the code thinks it wrote, (e) grep the codebase for column names against live schema. All five gate the next; cannot mark done with even one missing.

94. **Auto-scaffolded code is technical debt unless every line is read.** Code generators (Lovable, v0, Cursor agents, Claude Code's auto-scaffold) frequently write code against the schema they were trained on or the example schema in their prompt — not against the schema actually in your live database. ForgeMinds Phase 0 had ~600 lines of auto-scaffolded API routes referencing column names from an earlier mock schema. None matched the migrated schema. Mandatory after any auto-scaffold: (a) read every generated file end-to-end (not just diff), (b) grep all `.from(`, `.select(`, `.eq(`, `.insert(` calls and verify column names against `information_schema.columns`, (c) if a generator wrote files in `src/app/api/` and `src/lib/pipeline/`, treat the entire batch as suspect and run a per-file column audit before relying on any of it. Trust accumulated over time, not granted at scaffold-time.

95. **Definition of Done in markdown is decoration without mechanical enforcement.** Every VictorForge project has a Definition of Done section in vibe-standard.md and execution.md. ForgeMinds had it too. I declared "Phase 0 complete" three times anyway, because checklists in markdown can be skipped and there was no commit-time gate that rejected the claim. Lesson: every checklist needs a corresponding mechanical gate. Gate examples: (a) pre-commit hook rejecting commits whose message contains "done|complete|finished" without a corresponding AUDIT GATE block, (b) `npm run verify:phase-X` script that exits non-zero unless every checklist item programmatically passes, (c) per-phase checklist file in `.claude/checklists/` that pre-commit checks for 100% checked-off state. If a checklist exists only in vibe documentation, expect it to be skipped at the moment under pressure. If a checklist is enforced by code that blocks `git commit`, it gets followed. The cost of installing the gate is hours; the cost of "premature done" failure is days of debugging on a foundation that doesn't work.

96. **Schema drop+rebuild wipes role grants — Supabase JS SDK queries fail with 42501 even when migrations succeed.** When you reset a Supabase project schema with `drop schema public cascade`, the default privilege grants for `anon`, `authenticated`, and `service_role` roles are stripped. New tables created after the reset don't auto-grant to those roles, so JS SDK queries return PostgREST error 42501 ("permission denied") even though `npx supabase db push` (running as `postgres` superuser) succeeded. CLI works; web app fails. Mandatory after any `drop schema` for any reason: run a grants-restoration migration immediately. Required SQL: `grant usage on schema public + grant all on all tables/sequences/functions in schema public + alter default privileges for role postgres in schema public grant all on tables/sequences/functions to anon, authenticated, service_role`. Verify by running a query through the JS SDK before declaring schema done. ForgeMinds Phase 0 hit this exact failure mode after schema reset; spent an hour debugging "the keys are wrong" before finding it was actually missing grants.

## Multi-Tenant Discovery Discipline (from ForgeMinds Phase 1.5 pivot — May 2026)

97. **The "paste 10 URLs" reflex is a personal-pipeline mindset that doesn't survive multi-tenant.** *(ForgeMinds 2026-05-04.)* When building for one user, "paste your config" works because that user knows what they want. When building for thousands of users with wildly varied interests (medicine vs finance vs sports vs poetry), expertise (knows-what-RSS-is vs has-no-idea), budgets (free-only vs has-WSJ-subscription), and time (will-spend-an-hour-onboarding vs gives-up-after-30-seconds), the same UI fails because most users don't know which URLs to paste. The fix isn't a better form — it's a **conversational AI agent** that asks plain-language questions about what the user cares about, proposes options with reasoning ("I'm suggesting X because you mentioned Y"), and learns from feedback. Catch this whenever the implementation plan says "user adds X" without specifying *how the user discovers X*. If the next sentence isn't "via a curated catalog + AI suggestions" or "via OAuth-imported list from their existing account," then "user adds X" is a bug. See VIBE Rule 56 + factory CLAUDE.md §4 #18.

98. **Single-shared-API-key fetchers in multi-tenant apps burn quota for users who didn't ask.** *(ForgeMinds 2026-05-04.)* `/api/cron/ingest` was calling `fetchFinnhubNews()` + `fetchBenzingaNews()` + `fetchAlpacaNews()` + `fetchAlphaVantageNews()` unconditionally for every pipeline tick of every user — using a single shared API key per service. A medicine researcher with no interest in finance got billed against your Finnhub quota. A history teacher got Alpaca market data they never asked for. The architecture forced "every user gets all services." The fix: the route reads the user's `sources` table, groups by `type`, and only invokes fetchers for types the user has at least one row of. **Tripwire** for any multi-tenant route: count the number of API calls per request. If it's CONSTANT regardless of which user invoked, that's a bug — it means user prefs aren't gating the work. Constant per-request API count = global behavior, not per-user behavior.

## AI-at-the-Core Architecture (from VictorForge portfolio audit — May 2026)

99. **AI-as-bandage projects don't survive 2026+ competition.** *(Promoted from VictorForge portfolio audit 2026-05-05, after Dorsey-thesis review.)* Apps where AI is a side feature get out-competed by apps where AI is the core. Cursor outpaced VS Code+Copilot because Cursor was AI-first; VS Code is an editor with AI bolted on. Replit Agent outpaced traditional IDEs for the same reason. Lovable and v0 outpaced static scaffolders for the same reason. The pattern is universal: in any category where AI can produce real decisions/actions/outputs, the AI-at-the-center contender wins because the bandage version can't keep up with the speed of underlying model improvements. The mechanical test: if you removed every AI call from the app, what % would still work? ≥75% = bandage (will lose to AI-first competitor); ≤50% = at the core. Apply this audit at every project's V1 spec phase + at every phase close. If a project fails, it needs either a `DECISIONS.md` architectural justification or a redesign before V1. **Real failure mode:** BookDrop V1 (May 2026) was a document portal where bookkeepers manually categorized client uploads. Caught by this rule: Dext + Hubdoc + Receipt Bank are the AI-first incumbents in that exact category, all with auto-OCR + auto-categorization + auto-anomaly detection. Pure portal architecture would have lost on day 1 of launch. Pivoted before launch to AI-first: every upload runs through Gemini OCR → auto-categorization → auto-anomaly detection; bookkeeper reviews queue, doesn't sort from scratch. See VIBE Rule 57 + factory CLAUDE.md §4 #19.

100. **The "AI as marketing copy" trap.** *(VictorForge 2026-05-05.)* "AI-powered" in marketing copy with manual-form UI underneath is the most dangerous self-deception in 2026 product building. The user buys what the marketing claims; the AI-first competitor delivers what the marketing claims. The bandage product loses on the second visit. Auditing rule: if your landing page says "AI-powered X" but `grep -r "openai\|anthropic\|gemini" src/lib/` returns ≤5 call sites in core flows, the marketing is lying and the redesign is overdue. Specifically: AI used only for marketing-content generation (blog posts, social drafts) does NOT count as AI-at-core — it's AI-as-marketing-tool. The core flow is the user's actual job-to-be-done in the app, and that's where the AI must live.
